  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity vector $v.$ Differentiating the position vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}((t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}-36)^{3})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82(t-6){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t(t-6)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e82(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when its velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ \u27e82(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1},\\,6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}\u27e9 & =\u27e80,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-6)({t}^{2}-6t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=6,\\text{\u00a0}3\\pm 2\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ 6t({t}^{2}-36)^{2}=0\\, & \u21d2\\,t=0,\\pm 6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution $t=6$ is the only solution that satisfies both equations, so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
